From da09c985d1cada9d433b92b5752086e1ae7693af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 20:57:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Store real device keys, hand out one-time keys, carry
 to-device messages
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Matrix keeps the cryptography in the client; a homeserver's job for E2EE
is to remember which devices exist, hand out one one-time key per
request so a peer can open an Olm session, and deliver the to-device
message carrying the room key. None of that worked, so no client could
encrypt to anyone however complete its own crypto was.

The key directory was a single JSON blob, written only on the first
upload ever and filed under the literal device id "DEVICEID" whatever
the device called itself, so a second device was accepted and silently
discarded. It is now a KeyStore: device keys per user per real device
id, one-time keys alongside them, and cross-signing blobs kept
separately and echoed back on query. /keys/upload answers with the
counts it actually holds rather than a fixed 100 — a client uses those
counts to decide when to replenish, so an invented number means it
either never tops up or tops up forever.

/keys/claim is new: it pops one key of the requested algorithm per
device, so the same one-time key is never handed out twice, and answers
with the `failures` object the spec requires even when empty.

/sendToDevice is new, with delivery through /sync. The inbox is keyed by
recipient user rather than by device, because login still issues one
device id for everyone and the server cannot yet tell two of a user's
devices apart; on a mesh node — one user, one phone — those are the same
thing, and this wants revisiting when login issues real device ids.
Messages are removed as they are delivered, so a room key arrives once.

signatures_upload now merges into the device it signs rather than into a
fixed "DEVICEID" path, and skips a device it does not hold: a signature
over nothing means nothing.

Verified against a running server: two devices of one user are stored
and returned separately; two successive claims return different keys and
a third returns none; a to-device message sent to a user arrives in the
next /sync and is absent from the one after.
